      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we first need to determine the repeating decimal form of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\nStep 1: Perform the division of 1 by 7.\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{7} = 0.\\overline{142857}\n\\]\nThis means that the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is a repeating sequence of digits \"142857\". The repeating block is \"142857\", which has a length of 6 digits.\n\nStep 2: Determine the position of the 100th digit in the repeating sequence.\nTo find the 100th digit, we need to determine the position of 100 within the repeating cycle. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6.\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\nThis tells us that 100 is 4 more than a multiple of 6. Therefore, the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \"142857\".\n\nStep 3: Identify the 4th digit in the repeating sequence.\nThe repeating sequence \"142857\" has digits: 1, 4, 2, 8, 5, 7. The 4th digit is 2.\n\nThus, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
